"90_appspec_compat.mst — the names WINARMVM's tools were written against.

 `docs/appspec.md` §5.5. The acceptance test of this whole arc is the author's:
 the Windows sprite and sound editors should port over with NO EFFORT AT ALL.
 Their sources contain no Win32 whatsoever — verified, zero references — so the
 only thing standing between them and running here is that they say `WinSpec`
 and `WinToolWindow` where we say `AppSpec` and `AppToolWindow`.

 THE ALIAS MUST BE THE SUBCLASS, NEVER THE SUPERCLASS, and that direction is
 load-bearing rather than stylistic. Every type check inside the vendored model
 reads `isKindOf: AppNode`; an instance of a subclass answers true to that,
 while an AppNode asked `isKindOf: WinNode` would answer false and the differ
 would refuse every node it was handed. Verified before adopting: a node built
 through `WinSpec button:event:id:` is an AppNode, because the inherited
 constructor names AppNode explicitly, and it validates and diffs like any
 other.

 These are TRANSITIONAL. The better end state is the same rename in WINARMVM,
 after which this file is deleted; nothing depends on that happening, and until
 it does this is what keeps one tool source running on two platforms."

AppNode subclass: WinNode [ ]
AppSpec subclass: WinSpec [ ]
AppSpecLayout subclass: WinSpecLayout [ ]
AppToolWindow subclass: WinToolWindow [ ]
AppPixels subclass: WinPixels [ ]
AppRender subclass: WinRender [ ]
